    209 +## Phase 4: build the smallest useful agent hive
    210 +
    211 +Goal: demonstrate multiple agents with bounded authority, observable coordination, and reproducible replacement.
    212 +
    213 +Begin with three roles, each in a separate container rather than several agents sharing one mutable container:
    214 +
    215 +- a coordinator that accepts work and records desired state;
    216 +- one or two workers with separate writable workspaces;
    217 +- an optional narrow deployer that can build/check a proposed revision and request activation.
    218 +
    219 +Use a dedicated `.network` Quadlet and, only where useful, named `.volume` Quadlets. A `.pod` can be added when shared network namespace/lifecycle is genuinely helpful; it is not required merely because the services form a team.
    220 +
    221 +Default security boundary:
    222 +
    223 +- rootless Podman only;
    224 +- run as a non-root UID inside the image where practical;
    225 +- read-only root filesystem where practical;
    226 +- drop capabilities and use `no-new-privileges`;
    227 +- memory, CPU, PID, and restart limits;
    228 +- no host Podman socket;
    229 +- no host Docker socket;
    230 +- no writable host `/nix` and no access to the host Nix daemon socket;
    231 +- no broad `$HOME` mount;
    232 +- only explicit work, state, and secret paths;
    233 +- bind ports to loopback unless there is a reviewed need for LAN access.
    234 +
    235 +Do not put API keys, registry credentials, SSH keys, or tokens into Nix expressions, Home Manager `text` values, flake inputs, image layers, or Quadlet `Environment=` entries. Those are liable to become world-readable in the Nix store, image me
         tadata, generated unit, or process inspection. Inject secrets at runtime with permissions and lifecycle appropriate to the secret (for example Podman secrets or a host file mounted read-only), and keep their paths/content out of Git.
    236 +
    237 +The agents should administer **desired state**, not mutate their base image in place:
    238 +
    239 +```text
    240 +agent proposes change to the flake/config repository
    241 +  -> checks build in an unprivileged context
    242 +    -> policy/review gate accepts a known Git revision
    243 +      -> trusted deployer builds and activates that revision
    244 +        -> health check passes or deployment rolls back
    245 +```
    246 +
    247 +This gives agents a meaningful way to “administer their OS” while retaining history, reproducibility, and recovery. An agent running `nix profile install` or editing `/etc` inside an ephemeral container produces state that is hard to review and
          disappears on replacement.
    248 +
    249 +Exit gate:
    250 +
    251 +- Compromising one worker does not provide control of the host or other workers' private state.
    252 +- Every running image maps to a Git revision and locked Nix inputs.
    253 +- Agent-produced configuration must pass builds/checks before activation.
    254 +- A bad deployment automatically or manually rolls back to a known-good image/config.
    255 +- Logs, health checks, state backup, and secret rotation have all been exercised once.
    256 +
    257 +## Phase 5 decision: OCI workload or real self-administered NixOS?
    258 +
    259 +Make this decision only after the hive proof of concept.
    260 +
    261 +| Need | Best fit |
    262 +| --- | --- |
    263 +| Reproducible agent binaries and filesystem; one main process per unit | Nix-built OCI images under rootless Quadlet |
    264 +| Several cooperating services with isolated writable state | Several Quadlets plus a private Podman network/volumes |
    265 +| Agents propose changes and a controller rebuilds/redeploys | OCI + Git/flake control plane (recommended) |
    266 +| Agents need a package manager interactively for experiments | Disposable dev container or `nix develop`; do not confuse it with production state |
    267 +| Agents need their own systemd, users, NixOS modules, and `nixos-rebuild` | Dedicated NixOS VM, preferably with snapshots and resource limits |
    268 +| Untrusted agents require a strong isolation boundary | VM; a container shares the Fedora kernel |
    269 +
    270 +Running a full systemd-based NixOS userspace inside privileged Podman is technically possible in assorted forms, but it adds nested cgroups, privilege, mount, SELinux, and shutdown semantics precisely where a first deployment should stay simple
         . Native NixOS containers are also documented as NixOS-host functionality and are not perfectly isolated from their host. Neither is the preferred target for untrusted autonomous agents on Fedora.
    271 +
    272 +## Operational rules to adopt from day one
    273 +
    274 +1. Pin flake inputs and image digests; update them deliberately.
    275 +2. Build before activation, and inspect what will be linked/generated.
    276 +3. Separate immutable image content from writable state.
    277 +4. Back up named volumes/bind-mounted state; a Nix rebuild does not back up data.
    278 +5. Use health checks and bounded restart policies. Avoid infinite crash loops hidden by `Restart=always`.
    279 +6. Keep automatic image updates off until rollback is proven. A reproducible deployment should update by reviewed revision, not surprise timer.
    280 +7. Run syntax/unit verification and a smoke test in CI before deployment.
    281 +8. Never grant the hive a host container-engine socket as a shortcut to self-administration.
    282 +9. Keep the host runtime Fedora-native until there is a measured reason to replace it with Nixpkgs Podman.
    283 +10. Preserve at least one known-good Home Manager generation and image revision before garbage collection.
    284 +
    285 +## Proposed implementation order
    286 +
    287 +The next work should be split into small, independently reviewable changes:
    288 +
    289 +1. Add a smoke Quadlet and non-destructive deploy/undeploy scripts.
    290 +2. Run it manually, verify logs, then verify one reboot.
