  My recommendation is:

  - Keep Fedora responsible for Podman, systemd, SELinux, and the host OS.
  - Begin with a hand-written rootless Quadlet.
  - Then use Nix to build reproducible OCI images.
  - Introduce standalone, flake-pinned Home Manager later, initially managing only the Quadlet files.
  - Avoid Home Manager’s services.podman module at first because it also introduces Nixpkgs’ Podman and pre-generates systemd units, expanding the troubleshooting surface.
  - If agents genuinely need their own systemd, users, NixOS modules, and nixos-rebuild, use a NixOS VM rather than a privileged “NixOS-like” Podman container.

  The machine is already well prepared: rootless Podman 5.8.4, cgroup v2, user lingering, subordinate UID/GID ranges, overlay storage, the Quadlet generator, and enforcing SELinux are present. The degraded user-systemd status comes from three unrelated
  desktop helpers and is not a Quadlet blocker.

  One important conceptual distinction: Quadlet is a Podman/systemd facility (https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-systemd.unit.5.html), not a Nix feature. Nix’s role here is reproducible image and configuration construction. Likewise,
  installing Nix on Fedora does not make NixOS host modules available. The cautious Home Manager approach follows the project’s own recommendation to start with a small configuration (https://github.com/nix-community/home-manager#words-of-warning).

