    269 +  --disk path=/var/lib/libvirt/images/nixos-hive.qcow2,size=120,format=qcow2,bus=virtio,discard=unmap \
    270 +  --network network=default,model=virtio \
    271 +  --controller usb,model=qemu-xhci \
    272 +  --rng /dev/urandom \
    273 +  --channel unix,target_type=virtio,name=org.qemu.guest_agent.0 \
    274 +  --cdrom /var/lib/libvirt/images/iso/nixos-minimal-26.05-x86_64-linux.iso \
    275 +  --osinfo detect=on,require=off \
    276 +  --graphics none \
    277 +  --console pty,target_type=serial
    278 +```
    279 +
    280 +The NixOS ISO boot menu has a serial-console entry. Select it when installing through `virsh console`. If the current ISO/firmware combination gives a blank serial screen, do not weaken the VM or expose VNC publicly. Stop the VM, add local SPIC
         E graphics with virt-manager or `virsh edit`, complete installation through `virt-viewer`, and retain the serial device for the installed system.
    281 +
    282 +Detach from a `virsh console` session with **Ctrl+]**. This does not stop the VM.
    283 +
    284 +The VM created by this command will immediately appear in virt-manager because both use `qemu:///system`.
    285 +
    286 +## 6. Install NixOS inside the guest
    287 +
    288 +The commands in this section run **inside the NixOS installer VM**, not on the Fedora or Ubuntu host.
    289 +
    290 +### Confirm the target disk
    291 +
    292 +```bash
    293 +lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS
    294 +```
    295 +
    296 +With the settings above, the empty 120 GiB virtual disk should be `/dev/vda` and the ISO should be a separate read-only device. Confirm this carefully. The following partition commands erase their target, although in this design that target is
         only the new virtual disk.
    297 +
    298 +### Partition and mount for UEFI
    299 +
    300 +This simple layout has a 512 MiB EFI System Partition and one root filesystem. Add a separate data disk or swap later if measurements justify it.
    301 +
    302 +```bash
    303 +sudo parted /dev/vda -- mklabel gpt
    304 +sudo parted /dev/vda -- mkpart ESP fat32 1MiB 513MiB
    305 +sudo parted /dev/vda -- set 1 esp on
    306 +sudo parted /dev/vda -- mkpart root ext4 513MiB 100%
    307 +
    308 +sudo mkfs.fat -F 32 -n boot /dev/vda1
    309 +sudo mkfs.ext4 -L nixos /dev/vda2
    310 +
    311 +sudo mount /dev/disk/by-label/nixos /mnt
    312 +sudo mkdir -p /mnt/boot
    313 +sudo mount -o umask=077 /dev/disk/by-label/boot /mnt/boot
    314 +```
    315 +
    316 +### Generate the hardware configuration
    317 +
    318 +```bash
    319 +sudo nixos-generate-config --root /mnt
    320 +```
    321 +
    322 +Do not hand-copy `hardware-configuration.nix` from another system. It records the filesystems and detected virtual hardware for this VM.
    323 +
    324 +### Create the initial NixOS configuration
    325 +
    326 +Edit `/mnt/etc/nixos/configuration.nix` so it contains the following shape. Replace the example SSH key with the complete public key from the human administrator's host. Never put a private key or plaintext secret in this file.
    327 +
    328 +```nix
    329 +{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:
    330 +
    331 +{
    332 +  imports = [ ./hardware-configuration.nix ];
    333 +
    334 +  boot.loader.systemd-boot.enable = true;
    335 +  boot.loader.efi.canTouchEfiVariables = true;
    336 +
    337 +  # Keep the VM manageable through `virsh console` as well as a graphical console.
    338 +  boot.kernelParams = [
    339 +    "console=tty0"
    340 +    "console=ttyS0,115200n8"
    341 +  ];
    342 +
    343 +  networking.hostName = "nixos-hive";
    344 +  networking.useDHCP = lib.mkDefault true;
    345 +  time.timeZone = "America/New_York";
    346 +
    347 +  nix.settings.experimental-features = [ "nix-command" "flakes" ];
    348 +
    349 +  users.users.hiveadmin = {
    350 +    isNormalUser = true;
    351 +    description = "NixOS hive administrator";
    352 +    extraGroups = [ "wheel" ];
    353 +    openssh.authorizedKeys.keys = [
    354 +      "ssh-ed25519 REPLACE_WITH_THE_REAL_PUBLIC_KEY human-admin"
    355 +    ];
    356 +  };
    357 +
    358 +  services.openssh = {
    359 +    enable = true;
    360 +    settings = {
    361 +      PermitRootLogin = "no";
    362 +      PasswordAuthentication = false;
    363 +      KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false;
    364 +    };
    365 +  };
    366 +
    367 +  services.qemuGuest.enable = true;
    368 +  services.spice-vdagentd.enable = true;
    369 +
    370 +  virtualisation.podman.enable = true;
    371 +
    372 +  environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
    373 +    curl
    374 +    git
    375 +    jq
    376 +    tmux
    377 +    vim
    378 +  ];
    379 +
    380 +  # Set this to the release used for the first installation and do not
    381 +  # routinely change it during upgrades.
    382 +  system.stateVersion = "26.05";
    383 +}
    384 +```
    385 +
    386 +If this is intentionally a serial-only VM, `services.spice-vdagentd.enable` can be omitted. The QEMU guest agent remains useful because it lets libvirt query guest state and request clean shutdowns.
    387 +
    388 +### Make the configuration a pinned flake
    389 +
    390 +Create `/mnt/etc/nixos/flake.nix`:
    391 +
    392 +```nix
    393 +{
    394 +  description = "NixOS agent hive VM";
    395 +
    396 +  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-26.05";
    397 +
    398 +  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    399 +    {
    400 +      nixosConfigurations.nixos-hive = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
    401 +        system = "x86_64-linux";
    402 +        modules = [ ./configuration.nix ];
    403 +      };
    404 +    };
    405 +}
    406 +```
    407 +
    408 +When installing a later stable release, update the input and initial `system.stateVersion` together for the **first installation**. On subsequent upgrades, update the input deliberately but normally leave `system.stateVersion` at its original v
         alue.
    409 +
    410 +### Install and set a console password
    411 +
    412 +```bash
    413 +sudo nixos-install --flake /mnt/etc/nixos#nixos-hive
    414 +sudo nixos-enter --root /mnt -c 'passwd hiveadmin'
    415 +```
    416 +
    417 +The password is written interactively to the guest's password database; it is not exposed in the Nix store. `nixos-install` will also offer to set a root password. Once key-based access and sudo work, consider locking direct root password acces
         s inside the installed system with `sudo passwd -l root`.
    418 +
    419 +The installer should create `/mnt/etc/nixos/flake.lock`. Preserve and commit the lock file with the configuration after the first boot.
    420 +
    421 +### Reboot and remove the installer media
    422 +
    423 +Shut down from the installer rather than repeatedly booting the ISO:
    424 +
    425 +```bash
    426 +sudo poweroff
    427 +```
    428 +
    429 +Back on the host, find the CD-ROM target:
    430 +
    431 +```bash
    432 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domblklist nixos-hive --details
    433 +```
    434 +
    435 +Use the target name shown for the CD-ROM in place of `sda` if it differs:
    436 +
    437 +```bash
    438 +sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system change-media \
    439 +  nixos-hive sda --eject --config
    440 +sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system start nixos-hive
    441 +virsh --connect qemu:///system console nixos-hive
    442 +```
    443 +
    444 +In virt-manager, the equivalent action is **Remove Hardware** or **Disconnect** on the CD-ROM before starting the VM.
    445 +
    446 +## 7. Find the guest and connect over SSH
    447 +
    448 +Ask libvirt's DHCP lease table for the address:
    449 +
    450 +```bash
    451 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domifaddr nixos-hive --source lease
    452 +```
    453 +
    454 +If no address appears immediately, wait for DHCP and try again. Once the guest agent is running, this can also help:
    455 +
    456 +```bash
    457 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domifaddr nixos-hive --source agent
    458 +```
    459 +
    460 +Then connect from the host:
    461 +
    462 +```bash
    463 +ssh hiveadmin@GUEST_IP_ADDRESS
    464 +```
    465 +
    466 +Do not disable the NixOS firewall indiscriminately. The OpenSSH NixOS module opens its port when configured to do so by the current module defaults/options; verify inside the guest with `systemctl status sshd` and `ss -lntp` if connection fails
         .
    467 +
    468 +## 8. Day-to-day libvirt commands
    469 +
    470 +These commands run on the Fedora or Ubuntu host:
    471 +
    472 +```bash
    473 +# List VMs.
    474 +virsh --connect qemu:///system list --all
    475 +
    476 +# Start and connect to the serial console.
    477 +virsh --connect qemu:///system start nixos-hive
    478 +virsh --connect qemu:///system console nixos-hive
    479 +
    480 +# Ask the guest to shut down cleanly.
    481 +virsh --connect qemu:///system shutdown nixos-hive
    482 +
    483 +# Request a clean reboot.
    484 +virsh --connect qemu:///system reboot nixos-hive
    485 +
    486 +# Inspect virtual disks and NICs.
    487 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domblklist nixos-hive --details
    488 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domiflist nixos-hive
    489 +
    490 +# Start the VM when the host boots (optional).
    491 +sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system autostart nixos-hive
    492 +```
    493 +
    494 +Use `virsh destroy nixos-hive` only as the virtual equivalent of pulling a power cable. It is not a graceful shutdown and can damage guest filesystems or state.
    495 +
    496 +To resize CPU/RAM or edit devices, shut down the guest and use virt-manager or:
    497 +
    498 +```bash
    499 +sudo virsh --connect qemu:///system edit nixos-hive
    500 +```
    501 +
    502 +`virsh edit` validates the XML before saving, but malformed or incompatible device changes can still prevent boot. Save a copy first:
    503 +
    504 +```bash
    505 +virsh --connect qemu:///system dumpxml nixos-hive > nixos-hive.xml
    506 +```
    507 +
    508 +## 9. NixOS change, test, and rollback workflow
    509 +
    510 +Inside the guest, keep `/etc/nixos` in Git and commit `flake.lock`. A cautious change cycle is:
    511 +
    512 +```bash
    513 +cd /etc/nixos
    514 +
    515 +# Evaluate and build without activation.
    516 +sudo nixos-rebuild build --flake .#nixos-hive
    517 +
    518 +# Show the activation effects where supported.
    519 +sudo nixos-rebuild dry-activate --flake .#nixos-hive
    520 +
    521 +# Activate until reboot, but do not make it the default boot generation.
    522 +sudo nixos-rebuild test --flake .#nixos-hive
    523 +
    524 +# After validation, make it the active/default generation.
    525 +sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake .#nixos-hive
    526 +```
    527 +
    528 +If `test` breaks networking or services, rebooting returns to the previous boot generation. If a switched generation is bad, select an older generation in the boot menu or use the rollback support of `nixos-rebuild` after regaining access.
    529 +
    530 +Do not give an agent passwordless permission to invoke unrestricted `nixos-rebuild` while pretending that the agent is unprivileged. An agent that controls the evaluated NixOS module and can activate it effectively has root in the guest: it can
          create users, replace services, read secrets available to root, or install arbitrary activation scripts. That may be the desired meaning of “administer its own OS,” but it should be an explicit trust decision.
    531 +
    532 +A safer team workflow is:
    533 +
    534 +```text
    535 +agents edit a Git worktree as ordinary guest users
    536 +  -> Nix evaluation/build/checks run without activation
    537 +    -> a policy or human review approves a commit
    538 +      -> a narrow controller activates that exact commit
    539 +        -> health checks pass, or the VM/generation rolls back
    540 +```
    541 +
    542 +## 10. VM checkpoints, clones, and backups
    543 +
    544 +NixOS generations and VM backups solve different problems:
    545 +
    546 +- A NixOS generation rolls back declarative system software/configuration.
    547 +- A qcow2 snapshot/clone rolls back mutable data too.
    548 +- Neither is a substitute for an independent backup stored away from the host disk.
    549 +
    550 +Before a high-risk experiment, cleanly shut down the VM:
    551 +
    552 +```bash
    553 +virsh --connect qemu:///system shutdown nixos-hive
    554 +virsh --connect qemu:///system domstate nixos-hive
    555 +```
    556 +
    557 +A simple full clone can serve as a local known-good checkpoint:
    558 +
    559 +```bash
    560 +sudo virt-clone \
    561 +  --connect qemu:///system \
    562 +  --original nixos-hive \
    563 +  --name nixos-hive-known-good \
    564 +  --auto-clone
    565 +```
    566 +
    567 +Do not run the original and full clone on the same network without changing the clone's hostname, machine ID, and SSH host keys. A full clone copies those identities and any secrets on disk.
    568 +
    569 +Libvirt internal/external snapshots have storage-format, UEFI NVRAM, TPM-state, and guest-quiescing details that vary with the VM definition. Use them only after testing restoration. For a durable cold backup, shut the guest down and preserve:
    570 +
    571 +- `virsh dumpxml` output;
    572 +- every disk shown by `virsh domblklist`;
    573 +- UEFI NVRAM shown in the domain XML;
    574 +- virtual TPM state if a TPM was added;
    575 +- any external persistent volumes or network definitions.
    576 +
    577 +Keep backups outside `/var/lib/libvirt/images` and, ideally, off the host.
    578 +
    579 +## 11. Running the agent hive in NixOS
    580 +
    581 +Once the base VM and rollback path are proven, choose one of two guest layouts.
    582 +
    583 +### Agents as native NixOS services
    584 +
    585 +Use NixOS modules/systemd services when each agent is already a Nix package and does not need its own container boundary. This is the simplest way to let one NixOS configuration describe users, services, resource controls, directories, and netw
         orking.
    586 +
    587 +### Agents as Podman containers inside NixOS
    588 +
    589 +Keep `virtualisation.podman.enable = true` and choose one declarative interface:
    590 +
    591 +- NixOS `virtualisation.oci-containers` with `backend = "podman"` for system-level containers;
    592 +- raw Podman Quadlets under the appropriate rootful or rootless Quadlet path;
    593 +- later, a reviewed third-party Quadlet Nix module if its behavior is preferable.
    594 +
    595 +Start with the built-in NixOS `virtualisation.oci-containers` module or raw Quadlets rather than combining several container abstractions at once. Rootful system containers and per-agent rootless containers have different security and lifecycle
          tradeoffs; do not migrate between them accidentally.
    596 +
    597 +Even inside the VM:
    598 +
    599 +- keep agent data in explicit guest volumes/directories;
    600 +- keep secrets out of the Nix store and image layers;
    601 +- avoid mounting the host filesystem into the guest;
    602 +- do not pass the host Podman or libvirt socket through;
    603 +- use private virtual networks and loopback bindings until exposure is intentional;
    604 +- set CPU, memory, PID, and restart limits;
    605 +- back up mutable hive state independently of `/nix/store`.
    606 +
    607 +## 12. Optional: Nix's fast `build-vm` test loop
    608 +
    609 +The persistent libvirt VM should be the operational machine. Separately, Nix can build an ephemeral QEMU VM directly from a NixOS configuration. This is useful on either Fedora or Ubuntu for testing the configuration before applying it to the p
         ersistent guest.
    610 +
    611 +From a flake containing `nixosConfigurations.nixos-hive`:
    612 +
    613 +```bash
    614 +nix build \
    615 +  .#nixosConfigurations.nixos-hive.config.system.build.vm
    616 +./result/bin/run-nixos-hive-vm
    617 +```
    618 +
    619 +For a headless run with host port 2222 forwarded to guest SSH:
    620 +
    621 +```bash
    622 +QEMU_NET_OPTS="hostfwd=tcp:127.0.0.1:2222-:22" \
    623 +QEMU_KERNEL_PARAMS="console=ttyS0" \
    624 +  ./result/bin/run-nixos-hive-vm -nographic
    625 +```
    626 +
    627 +The NixOS module can give the test VM more resources without changing the real-machine configuration:
    628 +
    629 +```nix
    630 +virtualisation.vmVariant = {
    631 +  virtualisation = {
    632 +    memorySize = 8192;
    633 +    cores = 4;
    634 +  };
    635 +};
    636 +```
    637 +
    638 +This `build-vm` runner is not managed by libvirt, and its local qcow2 state can retain old users/data between runs. It is a test harness, not a replacement for the persistent VM, lifecycle management, backups, or the libvirt network.
    639 +
    640 +## 13. Troubleshooting checklist
    641 +
    642 +### `virt-host-validate` says KVM is unavailable
    643 +
    644 +- Check BIOS/UEFI VT-x or AMD-V/SVM.
    645 +- Check `/dev/kvm` and `kvm_intel`/`kvm_amd`.
    646 +- If the host is a VM, enable nested virtualization in the outer hypervisor.
    647 +- Verify the account's `kvm` group membership after a full logout/login.
    648 +
    649 +### `virsh` cannot connect
    650 +
    651 +- Always specify `--connect qemu:///system` while diagnosing; desktop tools may otherwise choose a session connection.
    652 +- On Fedora inspect `virtqemud.socket` and `journalctl -u virtqemud`.
    653 +- On Ubuntu inspect `libvirtd` and `journalctl -u libvirtd`.
    654 +- Do not solve socket errors by making libvirt sockets world-writable.
    655 +
    656 +### The default network is missing or inactive
    657 +
    658 +- Check `virsh --connect qemu:///system net-list --all`.
    659 +- Install/repair the distro's libvirt network configuration package.
    660 +- Check `virtnetworkd` on Fedora or `libvirtd` on Ubuntu.
    661 +- Check host firewall logs rather than disabling the firewall.
    662 +
    663 +### QEMU cannot read the ISO or disk
    664 +
    665 +- Use `/var/lib/libvirt/images` for system-libvirt media.
    666 +- On Fedora run `restorecon` instead of disabling SELinux.
    667 +- On Ubuntu keep AppArmor enabled and use its libvirt-supported paths.
    668 +- Confirm file and directory traversal permissions.
    669 +
    670 +### The serial console is blank
    671 +
    672 +- Use the ISO's serial-console boot entry.
    673 +- Confirm the VM has a serial PTY.
    674 +- Retain the `console=ttyS0,115200n8` guest kernel parameter.
    675 +- Use local SPICE/virt-viewer for installation if the ISO/UEFI menu is not visible on serial; do not expose unauthenticated VNC on `0.0.0.0`.
    676 +
    677 +### The VM boots the installer again
    678 +
    679 +- Power it off and eject/remove the virtual CD-ROM.
    680 +- Confirm the qcow2 disk is first in the persistent boot order.
    681 +
    682 +### The guest has no network
    683 +
    684 +- Confirm its NIC is attached to libvirt network `default` with model VirtIO.
    685 +- Confirm the network is active and DHCP has leases.
    686 +- Inspect `ip address`, `ip route`, and `systemctl status dhcpcd` inside NixOS.
    687 +- Do not immediately replace NAT with a host bridge; isolate the simpler failure first.
    688 +
    689 +### Builds consume too much disk
    690 +
    691 +- Inspect `df -h / /nix` and `nix path-info -Sh /run/current-system`.
    692 +- Preserve working generations before collecting garbage.
    693 +- Increase the qcow2 virtual size and then grow the guest partition/filesystem in a planned maintenance step.
    694 +- For a serious hive, consider a separate virtual disk for agent data so OS rollback and data backup have clearer boundaries.
    695 +
    696 +## 14. Proposed adoption sequence
    697 +
    698 +1. Install KVM/libvirt tools on the host and validate `/dev/kvm`.
    699 +2. Start the default NAT network.
    700 +3. Download and verify the NixOS ISO.
    701 +4. Create the VM with either virt-manager or `virt-install`.
    702 +5. Install the minimal flake-pinned NixOS configuration.
    703 +6. Verify serial console, SSH, clean shutdown, and guest-agent reporting.
    704 +7. Commit the guest's `/etc/nixos` configuration and lock file to a private/reviewed repository.
    705 +8. Make a powered-off known-good clone or cold backup.
    706 +9. Add one native service or one Podman workload—not the entire agent hive.
    707 +10. Exercise NixOS `build`, `test`, `switch`, boot-generation rollback, and VM restoration.
    708 +11. Add the coordinator and workers with explicit resource and authority boundaries.
    709 +12. Enable host autostart only after shutdown and recovery behavior is understood.
